The widget lives on the dashboard, not in your firmware. On the device you handle
writes to that variable with a `NODRIX_WRITE` handler — it fires whenever the cloud
writes `pump`:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("pump") {
  digitalWrite(PUMP_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

You never construct the widget in code and there is no `addWidget()`. The dashboard owns
the widget; the device owns the reaction.

No. There is no `Toggle` class and no `Nodrix.addWidget()`. Widgets are created on the
dashboard and bound to a variable name. Firmware only reacts to writes of that variable,
with `NODRIX_WRITE`:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("relay") {
  digitalWrite(RELAY_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>

const int FAN_PIN = 5;

NODRIX_WRITE("fan") {
  digitalWrite(FAN_PIN, value.asBool());
}

void setup() {
  pinMode(FAN_PIN, OUTPUT);
  Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
}

void loop() {
  Nodrix.run();
}
```

Read it inside a `NODRIX_WRITE("speed")` handler with the accessor that matches the type:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("speed") {
  if (value.isNull()) return;
  analogWrite(MOTOR_PIN, constrain(value.asInt(), 0, 255));
}
```

Guard with `isNull()` so a device connecting before the slider is set doesn't apply 0.

You don't bind in firmware — you match a name. Create the widget on the dashboard bound
to, say, `light`, then write a handler for the same name on the device:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("light") {
  digitalWrite(LIGHT_PIN, value.asBool());
}
```

The shared variable name is the binding. There is no registration call.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>
#include <esp_sleep.h>

#define SLEEP_HOURS 1

void setup() {
  Nodrix.beginHTTP(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
  Nodrix.send("battery_mv", analogReadMilliVolts(35));
  Nodrix.flush();
  Nodrix.poll();
  esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup((uint64_t)SLEEP_HOURS * 3600ULL * 1000000ULL);
  esp_deep_sleep_start();
}

void loop() {}
```

A device that wakes, reports and sleeps uses HTTP mode: `beginHTTP()` with `poll()`.
Never `run()` here — there is no persistent socket to service.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>
#include <esp_sleep.h>

#define SLEEP_MINUTES 30

void setup() {
  Nodrix.beginHTTP(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
  Nodrix.send("soil", analogRead(34));
  Nodrix.flush();
  Nodrix.poll();
  esp_sleep_enable_timer_wakeup((uint64_t)SLEEP_MINUTES * 60ULL * 1000000ULL);
  esp_deep_sleep_start();
}

void loop() {}
```

No. Commands are queued and delivered at-least-once. They apply on your next `Nodrix.poll()`,
which runs your `NODRIX_WRITE` handlers for anything that arrived while you slept:

```cpp
NODRIX_WRITE("interval_min") {
  if (!value.isNull()) sleepMinutes = value.asInt();
}
```

So a command sent during a nap is applied on the next wake, not lost.

```cpp
#include <Nodrix.h>

unsigned long lastSend = 0;

void setup() {
  Nodrix.begin(WIFI_SSID, WIFI_PASS, HOST, TOKEN);
}

void loop() {
  Nodrix.run();

  if (millis() - lastSend > 5000) {
    lastSend = millis();
    Nodrix.send("uptime_s", (long)(millis() / 1000));
  }
}
```
